.TH CONVEN 1 "August 28, 2026" "Conven 0.3.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conven \- run a focused set of local microservices with remote dependencies
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBconven\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIpath\fR]... \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR]
\fBconven\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIpath\fR]... \fBhelp\fR [\fIcommand\fR]
\fBconven\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIpath\fR]... [\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR]
\fBconven\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIpath\fR]... [\fB\-v\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B conven
is a local microservice launcher.
It starts selected services from a workspace, keeps unselected dependencies
reachable through the configured development infrastructure, materializes
runtime configuration outside the source tree, and records the local session
under
.IR <workspace>/.conven/runtime/current .
.PP
Conven discovers the nearest canonical
.I .conven
workspace from the effective working directory.
The workspace manifest declares services, environments, connections, runners,
ports, dependencies, and policy-backed configuration adapters.
.PP
For typed local services, startup fails closed unless the selected policy can
prove that remote service registration is disabled or not applicable and that
the service listener is restricted to a loopback address.
Conven does not create a reverse route from the remote cluster to local
services.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-C " path"
Run as if Conven was started in
.I path
instead of the current working directory.
This option may be repeated. A relative path is resolved against the effective
working directory established by any preceding
.B \-C
option.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Initialize a workspace in the effective working directory.
Conven scans direct-child Git repositories for supported service layouts and
creates
.IR .conven/conven.yaml .
This scan is the initial registry pass. Initialization also creates missing
.IR .conven/catalog.yaml ,
.IR CONVEN-WORKSPACE-POLICY-GENERATOR-AI-SPEC.md ,
and
.IR README.md
without overwriting existing files.
An existing manifest is not overwritten.
Use
.BR "init \-\-local"
to create a Manifest v2 no-cluster environment. Conven adds no infrastructure,
secret files, or Docker requirement. Start databases and brokers with project
tooling, then declare their reachable addresses as environment endpoints.
.TP
.BR config " [" \-\-global "] [" \-\-list | \-\-unset "] [" key "] [" value "]"
Read, write, list, or remove Conven settings.
Without
.BR \-\-global ,
reads combine global and workspace settings while writes affect only the
workspace configuration.
The valid forms are:
.nf
    conven config [\-\-global] \-\-list
    conven config [\-\-global] key
    conven config [\-\-global] key value
    conven config [\-\-global] \-\-unset key
.fi
.TP
.B catalog
Edit or validate the declarative service catalog at
.IR <workspace>/.conven/catalog.yaml .
Exactly one of
.BR \-\-edit
or
.BR \-\-validate
is required. Edit publishes a temporary copy only after strict validation.
.TP
.B policy
Edit, import, or reset the canonical workspace manifest.
Exactly one of
.BR \-\-edit ,
.BR \-\-import ,
or
.B \-\-reset
is required.
.TP
.B plugins
Install, list, remove, or run Python plugins. The default workspace scope is
.IR <workspace>/.conven/plugins ;
.B --global
selects
.IR ~/.conven/plugins .
Project-specific plugins are not implicitly bundled with Conven.
Relative install paths are resolved from the effective working directory.
Use
.BR "\-\-install [\-\-global] python-file" ,
.BR "\-\-list [\-\-global]" ,
.BR "\-\-remove [\-\-global] name" ,
or
.BR "\-\-run [name] [plugin-args...]" .
Use
.BR "plugins \-\-global \-\-run name [plugin-args...]"
to require the user-global plugin with an explicit name; the older
.BR "plugins \-\-run \-\-global name"
ordering is also accepted.
The unqualified list is grouped by workspace and global scope. An explicit
name prefers workspace and warns before global fallback. An omitted name runs
the sole workspace plugin with a warning, opens a single-selection picker when
multiple workspace plugins exist, or opens the picker for global candidates
when no workspace plugin exists. Global candidates carry a highlighted
.B global
label; even a sole global candidate requires selection and confirmation.
An explicitly global run always requires
.IR name .
The grouped list requires a workspace; outside one, use
.BR "plugins \-\-list \-\-global" .
Generator options are passed through unchanged. Compatible policy generators
accept
.BR "\-\-output [file]"
(with no file, use
.IR <workspace>/application.yaml )
and
.BR "\-\-disable-bindings binding..." ;
the plugin, not Conven, owns overwrite confirmation.
An immediate
.B \-\-global
after
.B \-\-run
selects the global scope; use
.B \-\-
before it to pass that argument to an automatically selected plugin.
Conven injects the reserved
.B \-\-workspace
argument, which plugin arguments cannot override.
.TP
.B services
List, start, restart, stop, clean, and inspect services in the effective
workspace.
See
.B SERVICES ACTIONS
below.
.TP
.B status
Show the complete workspace overview: workspace and manifest paths, declared
services with types and ports, effective configured
.BR ktctl.path / ktctl.kubeconfig
values, configured environment endpoints, disabled bindings from the catalog,
and the saved runtime session state, including the hot-reload watcher.
.TP
.B doctor
Validate the resolved workspace, selected environment, connection settings,
and static startup configuration without starting services.
.TP
.BR help " [" command "]"
Show the root command overview or detailed help for one command.
For example,
.B conven help services
is equivalent to
.BR "conven services \-\-help" .
.TP
.B version
Print a compact Conven ASCII mark followed by the version, release date, and
project homepage:
.nf
    conven version VERSION (YYYY-MM-DD)
    https://github.com/leo1394/homebrew-conven
.fi
The mark uses terminal colors when available and remains plain ASCII when color
is disabled.
The
.BR \-v " and " \-\-version
forms are equivalent.
.SH SERVICES ACTIONS
The action flag must be the first argument after
.BR "conven services" .
.TP
.B \-\-list
List services declared by the workspace manifest.
.TP
.B "\-\-registry [\-\-prune]"
Conservatively update services from direct-child repositories.
Existing non-empty manual declarations take precedence.
With
.BR \-\-prune ,
remove manifest services whose repositories no longer exist.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Show the saved local session, hot-reload watcher, service PID and PGID
information, and relevant shared connection state.
.TP
.B "\-\-dashboard [service...]"
Open the full-screen Dashboard for the current session and optional service
selection.
The banner lists disabled bindings and places the earliest start time of
the displayed services at the far right of the title.
The equivalent alias is
.BR "services \-\-logs \-\-dashboard [service...]" .
Its fixed banner remains visible while the application retains and scrolls up
to 10,000 lines received by the viewer. The initial replay budget is shared
across selected log sources; subsequent lines are retained in arrival order.
Long logical log lines wrap to the terminal width and can be scrolled by visual
row instead of being replaced by a viewport ellipsis.
Use the mouse wheel, Up/Down, or PgUp/PgDn to browse, Home or
.B g
for the oldest retained line, and End or
.B G
to resume live follow mode.
Use
.B /
to search,
.BR n / N
to move between matches, and Esc to clear the search.
Press
.B q
or Ctrl-C to detach without stopping services.
This application-level search is independent of terminal search; Command+F can
only inspect the currently rendered Dashboard frame.
If the terminal is unsuitable for the Dashboard, the action fails with guidance
to use
.BR "services \-\-logs \-\-tail" .
.TP
.B "\-\-logs [\-\-tail] [\-\-dashboard] [service...]"
Show logs for the current session.
With
.BR \-\-tail ,
follow new output in a plain continuous stream on the terminal primary screen.
The stream enters native terminal scrollback and can be searched with
Command+F, subject to the terminal profile's scrollback limit.
On attachment, an initial replay budget of up to 10,000 lines is shared across
the selected log sources before following new output.
Use Ctrl-C to detach from the plain stream without stopping services;
.B q
is a Dashboard-only control.
With
.BR \-\-dashboard ,
open the same full-screen viewer as the independent Dashboard action.
If both mode options appear, they are evaluated from left to right and the last
one selects the effective mode.
.TP
.B "\-\-start [options] [service...]"
Start a fresh local service session.
Without service names, open the interactive selector and require confirmation.
By default only named or selected business services are started. Use
.B \-\-with\-dependencies
to include their transitive local service dependencies. Databases, brokers,
and other infrastructure are external to Conven and are never started by this
option.
If a verified session is already running, an interactive start first validates
the replacement plan, then offers
.B "Stop then start"
or
.BR Cancel ;
Cancel is the default.
A non-interactive conflict fails closed and leaves the current session
unchanged. Unverified processes or connections are never eligible for automatic
replacement. After confirmation, Conven rechecks the session under the
workspace lock and refuses replacement if it changed. A compatible, available
managed
.B ktctl
lease is reused without interrupting the connection.
After safety and static validation, Conven rebuilds
.IR runtime/current ,
checks referenced endpoints, establishes the configured connection,
materializes configuration, prepares and builds services, starts them in
dependency order, and performs health checks.
After a successful start, Conven opens the Dashboard automatically when attached
to a usable interactive terminal.
Without such a terminal, start returns and leaves the services running unless
.B \-\-tail
was explicitly requested.
Successful starts also launch a workspace hot-reload watcher. Source changes
are debounced and rebuild only affected running services. Compiler output is
copied to the build log and service log. A failed build leaves the
last-known-good process running; only a successful build and preflight enter
the controlled stop/start handoff.
.TP
.B "\-\-restart [options] [service...]"
Restart named services, or detect changed and exited services in the current
session when no names are supplied.
The fixed runtime directory and existing connection are reused; unchanged
service artifacts, configuration, processes, and logs remain untouched.
On a usable interactive terminal, restart opens the Dashboard after completing.
Without such a terminal it returns and leaves services running. Use
.B \-\-tail
to follow selected logs in the plain stream, or all current-session logs when
no service names were supplied.
Use
.B \-\-dashboard
to request the Dashboard explicitly. If both mode options appear, the last one
selects the effective mode.
Restart always reuses the current session environment. Passing
.BR \-\-dev ,
.BR \-\-test ,
or
.B \-\-env
prints a one-line warning and points to the corresponding
.B services \-\-start
command before showing restart usage.
Successful restarts ensure that the same workspace hot-reload watcher remains
active. The watcher is terminated by
.BR "services \-\-stop\-all" .
.TP
.B "\-\-stop [\-\-force] (service...|\-\-all)"
Stop selected services or the entire effective workspace session.
Stopping all services releases the workspace connection lease but preserves
.I runtime/current
until the next fresh start.
.TP
.B "\-\-stop\-all [\-\-force]"
Exact shorthand for
.BR "conven services \-\-stop \-\-all" .
.TP
.B \-\-cleanup
Remove saved files under
.I runtime/current/artifacts
and
.IR runtime/current/logs .
Cleanup refuses while a session is saved; run
.B conven services \-\-stop\-all
first. Materialized runtime configs and the shared connection log are
preserved.
.SH START, RESTART, AND DOCTOR OPTIONS
The following options are available to
.B services \-\-start
and, where noted, to restart or
.BR doctor .
Use action-specific
.B \-\-help
for the authoritative option list.
.TP
.BI \-\-env " name"
Select a manifest environment profile.
When omitted, Conven selects
.B dev
when present, otherwise the sole profile, otherwise
.B local
when present. Ambiguous profiles require an explicit value.
Available to start and doctor.
.TP
.B \-\-dev
Select the
.B dev
profile.
Available to start and doctor.
.TP
.B \-\-test
Select the
.B test
profile.
Available to start and doctor.
.TP
.BI \-\-kubeconfig " file"
Override the Kubernetes configuration path.
Available to start and doctor.
.TP
.BI \-\-context " name"
Override the Kubernetes context.
Available to start and doctor.
.TP
.BI \-\-namespace " name"
Override the Kubernetes namespace used by the connection.
Available to start and doctor.
.TP
.B \-\-tail
Follow aggregated service logs in a plain continuous stream after a successful
start or restart, overriding their automatic TTY Dashboard.
Also accepted by
.BR "services \-\-logs" .
It is a boolean switch, not a line-count option.
.TP
.B \-\-dashboard
Open the interactive Dashboard after restart. Also accepted by
.BR "services \-\-logs" .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Resolve and validate a start plan without creating or modifying runtime state,
requesting remote configuration, establishing a connection, building, or
starting processes.
.TP
.B \-\-with\-dependencies
Expand the start selection with transitive local service dependencies and start
the resulting dependency-ordered group. Available only to
.BR "services \-\-start" .
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-build
Skip service builds.
A fresh start cannot reuse default artifacts inside
.IR runtime/current ;
restart can reuse current artifacts.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-verify
Skip service health checks.
.SH CONFIGURATION AND POLICY
The canonical workspace manifest is
.IR <workspace>/.conven/conven.yaml .
Manifest version 1 retains the 0.2.x environment and local/remote service
dependency behavior. Manifest version 2 adds environment files, endpoints,
and explicit environment resolutions without managing infrastructure.
There is no separate policy file.
Policy profiles in the manifest describe framework, configuration source,
service discovery, materialization, routing, and local-isolation rules.
.PP
.B conven policy \-\-edit
publishes a temporary edited copy only after strict validation.
.B conven policy \-\-import
installs an entire local YAML file, backs up the previous manifest, and does not
merge it with repository scan results. If the filename is omitted, Conven scans
regular
.I .yaml
and
.I .yml
files directly under the workspace root and opens a single-selection picker,
even when only one candidate exists. Non-interactive callers must pass the
filename explicitly.
.B conven policy \-\-reset
destructively rebuilds the whole manifest from facts that built-in analyzers can
prove; manual rules must be restored from the backup or declared again.
.PP
Run
.BR "conven doctor" " and " "conven services \-\-start \-\-dry\-run"
after importing or materially changing a policy.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <workspace>/.conven/conven.yaml
Canonical workspace manifest.
.TP
.I <workspace>/.conven/config
Workspace-local settings.
.TP
.I <workspace>/.conven/plugins
Workspace-local Python plugins; this scope takes precedence over global plugins.
.TP
.I <workspace>/.conven/catalog.yaml
User- or AI-reviewed policy-generator catalog. A service declares
.IR repository ,
.IR rpcBinding ,
or both, together with
.I kind
and a unique local
.IR port .
The
.I disabledRpcBindings
list keeps selected bindings remote.
.TP
.I <workspace>/CONVEN-WORKSPACE-POLICY-GENERATOR-AI-SPEC.md
AI-readable policy-generator implementation and validation contract created
during initialization.
.TP
.I <workspace>/conven-generator.json
Workspace-specific generator profile, environment, and connection input.
Conven does not generate this file because those policy values cannot be
inferred safely.
.TP
.I <workspace>/README.md
No-clobber workspace quick start created by initialization when absent.
.TP
.I <workspace>/.conven/.gitignore
Workspace-local ignore rules; initialization non-destructively adds the runtime
directory.
.TP
.I <workspace>/.conven/backups
Backups created before manifest import or reset publication.
.TP
.I <workspace>/.conven/runtime
Private workspace runtime root containing the lock, service session state, and
connection log.
.TP
.I <workspace>/.conven/runtime/current
Current artifacts, materialized configurations, and service logs.
.B services \-\-cleanup
removes only the artifacts and service logs after the session has stopped.
.TP
.I ~/.conven/config
User-global settings.
.TP
.I ~/.conven/plugins
Installed user-global plugins.
.TP
.I ~/.conven/state/connections
Cross-workspace shared connection lease metadata.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CONVEN_EDITOR
Editor command used by
.BR "policy \-\-edit"
and
.BR "catalog \-\-edit" .
Falls back to
.BR VISUAL ,
then
.BR EDITOR ,
then
.BR vi .
.TP
.B CONVEN_KUBECONFIG
Preferred kubeconfig override when no command-line override is supplied.
.TP
.B KTCTL_KUBECONFIG
Compatibility kubeconfig override used after
.BR CONVEN_KUBECONFIG .
.TP
.B KUBECONFIG
Standard Kubernetes fallback.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colored terminal output when set.
.TP
.B TERM
When set to
.BR dumb ,
disable color and the interactive log dashboard.
.PP
Conven injects runtime metadata into service, prepare, and build processes
using variables including
.BR CONVEN_WORKSPACE ,
.BR CONVEN_SERVICE ,
.BR CONVEN_ENV ,
.BR CONVEN_STATE_DIR ,
.BR CONVEN_RUN_DIR ,
.BR CONVEN_CONFIG_DIR ,
.BR CONVEN_ARTIFACT ,
and
.BR CONVEN_SELECTED_SERVICES .
These variables support orchestration and runner hooks. Typed application source
should consume the framework-native argument declared by its trusted adapter;
.B CONVEN_CONFIG_DIR
is not the required application integration API.
Plugins receive the canonical workspace through
.BR CONVEN_WORKSPACE .
.SH SERVICE RUNTIME CONFIGURATION
A typed service must accept the runtime-configuration argument declared by its
adapter and actually load configuration from that exact path. Merely receiving,
declaring, or ignoring the argument does not satisfy the Conven contract.
A missing path, invalid path, or configuration parse failure must terminate the
service with a non-zero status. The service must not silently fall back to a
repository-default configuration.
.PP
Conven does not require every language to use the same flag. The current trusted
go-zero and Consul adapter requires exactly one
.B \-f
argument whose value is the verified absolute runtime configuration directory.
Other adapters should use their framework-native argument, for example Spring
Boot's
.BR \-\-spring.config.location .
The manifest or policy expands
.B ${configDir}
into that argument; business source need not reference Conven-specific names.
.PP
Static command validation proves that the adapter argument points to the guarded
runtime directory. Runtime health and adapter contract tests must additionally
prove that the service consumed it, including a listener canary that differs
from the repository default.
.SH SAFETY
For typed services, the verified adapter contract prevents selected services
from being advertised through the configured remote service registry and
limits their listeners to loopback.
It does not isolate business data or messaging.
Local services may still connect to remote databases, Kafka, RPC endpoints, and
other infrastructure, and may write data or consume messages.
.PP
Use
.B \-\-force
only after verifying the saved PID and PGID.
It bypasses normal process identity checks during stop recovery.
.SH NOTES
Service lifecycle actions are flags under the
.B services
command.
Use
.BR "conven services \-\-start" ,
not
.B conven start
or
.BR "conven services start" .
The former top-level service lifecycle commands
.BR list ,
.BR discover ,
.BR logs ,
.BR start ,
.BR restart ,
and
.B stop
have been removed.
The top-level
.B status
command is an aggregate workspace overview; runtime-only status remains
.BR "services \-\-status" .
The former
.B \-\-follow
option is now
.BR \-\-tail .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, displayed help, or an explicit interactive cancellation.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, validation, connection, build, process, or runtime failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid syntax, unknown command, unknown action, or unsupported flag.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
conven init
conven \-C /path/of/workspace services \-\-start \-\-test service1 service2
conven help services
conven \-\-version
conven catalog \-\-validate
conven catalog \-\-edit
conven policy \-\-edit
conven status
conven doctor \-\-test \-\-namespace test
conven services \-\-start \-\-dry\-run api service-a
conven services \-\-start \-\-test \-\-namespace test \-\-tail api service-a
conven services \-\-restart
conven services \-\-logs \-\-tail api
conven services \-\-stop\-all
conven services \-\-cleanup
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR brew (1),
.BR git (1),
.BR kubectl (1)
